WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.650
Joker, the much-anticipated new take on the
DC Comics villain of the same name, weaves

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:11.000
such a winding narrative that it leaves behind
plenty of fascinating implications, and huge

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.520
questions, after the credits roll.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:14.340
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.040
Let's get this one out of the way first.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.400
Arthur Fleck spends a lot of time lost in
fantasy.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.020
We see this almost immediately in the film,
when he's watching Murray Franklin and fantasizes

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.500
about being an audience member who gets called
down in front of the cameras.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.360
Later, this goes even further when we realize
that Arthur has imagined an entire romantic

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.740
relationship with his neighbor Sophie.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:41.100
This tendency toward fantasy, coupled with
things like the dreamlike way he walks out

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.409
of the interview room in the final shot of
the film, are enough to make us wonder how

00:00:45.409 --> 00:00:48.300
much of the story takes place in Arthur's
head.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.180
We know for sure that some elements are imagined,
but what else might be?

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.700
Did he ever actually make it out of that cop
car in the middle of the riot?

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.340
Did he actually kill his therapist?

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.660
Was he even, perhaps, in the asylum the whole
time?

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:08.170
It's hard to be certain, but the rest of these
questions rely on at least some of these events

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:08.820
being true.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.320
Arthur Fleck's life is unraveling from pretty
much the moment Joker begins, but things only

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:19.380
get worse when he learns that his mother Penny
believes that Thomas Wayne, her former employer,

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:20.540
is Arthur's father.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.690
At first, Arthur has no reason to believe
his mother is wrong, but when he confronts

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:29.480
Wayne about it, the billionaire insists that
Penny is just delusional.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.620
Later, Arthur recovers paperwork from Arkham
State Hospital that apparently confirms that

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.720
he was adopted, but is that the whole story?

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.900
Even later in the film, he finds a photograph
of his mother that Thomas Wayne apparently

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:46.900
inscribed on the back with a comment about
Penny's lovely smile... so how much of the

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.360
Wayne story was really true?

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:54.570
Joker is a film that keeps most of its very
precise focus on Arthur Fleck and his very

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:59.070
small life, but he's accidentally a catalyst
for a much larger picture.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:03.200
He tells Murray Franklin that he doesn't consider
himself political, but the subway murders

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.380
he commits spark a sweeping political movement,
and by the end of the film, that movement

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:13.540
considers him its leader, cheering at his
feet in the streets of a burning Gotham.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:18.480
While this de facto Joker Gang was founded
on anti-rich sentiment amid worsening conditions

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.110
for the working class in Gotham City, by the
end of the film their protests have turned

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:24.340
into full-on riots.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.980
This begs the question: are they ever actually
able to achieve anything?

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:34.120
Does the murder of someone as important as
Thomas Wayne drive them back into the shadows...

00:02:34.129 --> 00:02:36.099
or do they become more active than ever?

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:42.549
A key subplot in Joker follows Thomas Wayne's
campaign to become mayor of Gotham City, and

00:02:42.549 --> 00:02:45.320
his criticisms of the demonstrators who revere
the subway shooter.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:50.320
Wayne is portrayed in the film as a powerful,
blustery man who happens to be sitting atop

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:54.720
the powder keg that is Gotham City politics,
and he's more than just a little oblivious

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.160
to the lives of the less fortunate.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:01.940
But no matter how out of touch Thomas Wayne
is, why on earth would he take his wife and

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:06.510
young son to a movie theater in the middle
of Gotham on the night of a planned protest

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:07.300
against the rich?

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:13.000
And even if he did want to take that risk,
why didn't he at least have a driver waiting

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:13.980
to escort them out?

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.900
Whether courageous or just clueless, it seems
like a really bad time to go to the movies

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:20.920
when you've got a perfectly good mansion to
chill in.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:26.870
There have been many similar versions of Batman's
origin story, but they all involve the death

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.629
of Bruce Wayne's parents, whether they're
gunned down by a random mugger, or a guy specifically

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:33.620
named Joe Chill or Jack Napier.

00:03:34.189 --> 00:03:39.019
In Joker, the Waynes are killed by a random,
clown-masked rioter who happens to have a

00:03:39.019 --> 00:03:43.299
gun, but there's no way Bruce Wayne won't
later learn that the whole ordeal happened

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:44.220
because of Arthur Fleck.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:49.860
With that in mind, is Batman's eventual mission
to fight crime cast in a different light?

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:55.040
It's no longer just "crime" that killed his
parents; it's the violent movement surrounding

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:55.600
Joker.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.640
In this world, does Bruce Wayne still become
Batman, or does his mission change?

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.540
Will he focus on Arthur Fleck exclusively?

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.740
And if he does become Batman, how does knowing
what he knows about Arthur change him?

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.000
Arthur Fleck is at first reluctant to embrace
the violence within him, but by the end of

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.320
Joker, the dam has clearly broken.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:20.320
"All I have are negative thoughts."

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.340
Arthur is no longer interested in holding
himself back, from killing Murray Franklin

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.320
on live TV, to murdering the therapist interviewing
him at Arkham.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:32.720
As the film ends, Arthur's potential escape
is left up in the air.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.520
Because he's the Joker now, we can reasonably
assume that he did manage to get out of Arkham

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.320
that day, and is on the loose again after
the credits roll.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.260
If that happened, what's his purpose now?

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:46.380
Who does he go after next?

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:50.080
His mother's old abusive boyfriend, or his
birth parents?

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:55.240
Or will he become like the comic book version
of the Joker, and simply act as an agent of

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.260
chaos, pursuing random bloodshed?

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.120
Whatever he does, it won't be pretty.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:05.920
Gotham City is a metropolis teetering on the
brink even before Arthur Fleck's rampage.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:11.470
It's dirty, covered in graffiti, and mountains
of garbage are piling up amid a strike while

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:14.260
super rats stalk the darker parts of town.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.580
It's a city infested with crime, unemployment,
and anger.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.840
The rise of Arthur and those who sympathize
with him only makes things worse.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.840
It all seems to culminate in the murders of
Thomas Wayne and Murray Franklin, but surely

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.400
that's not where it ends.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:32.880
With Thomas Wayne dead, the divide between
the privileged and the underprivileged in

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.280
Gotham could deepen very quickly.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:38.980
The police could crack down even harder on
people who don't deserve it, and the Joker

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:41.640
Gang could respond with even bigger, more
violent riots.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:46.600
The city is on edge, and it won't have Batman
to come to the rescue for at least another

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:47.160
decade.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.140
So what does Gotham do?

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.480
How does it cope?

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:51.940
There's no easy answer.

